    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `membershipTier` is a value *derived* from an owner's own fields, so it belongs alongside the other computed read-only owner attributes (`displayName`, `initials`, `membershipNumber`) rather than in the request pipeline.\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipTier` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and it serializes into the `GET /api/owners/{id}` response.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"membershipTier\", expression = \"java(membershipTier(owner))\")` and a small `membershipTier(Owner)` default method holding the rule:\n\n```java\ndefault String membershipTier(Owner owner) {\n    Integer namesakeCount = owner.getNamesakeCount();\n    String email = owner.getEmail();\n    boolean silver = namesakeCount != null && namesakeCount == 0 && email != null && !email.isBlank();\n    return silver ? \"SILVER\" : \"BRONZE\";\n}\n```\n\nThis mirrors the existing `membershipNumber` derivation (same single-responsibility helper + expression-mapping pattern), reusing the mapper rather than adding a new pipeline step or duplicating logic. `namesakeCount` is already computed at registration by `CountNamesakes`, so the tier reads straight from the stored value.\n\nBoth new acceptance tests pass: SILVER for a unique owner with an email, BRONZE for an owner with no email.",
